{
    "model": "puzzles.chesspuzzlecategory",
    "pk": 4,
    "fields": {
        "slug": "pin-a-piece",
        "name": "Pin a piece",
        "description": "Pin an enemy piece to its king or queen.",
        "prompt": "You are a chess trainer. Compose {{ count }} original \"pin a piece\" puzzles.\n\nIn these puzzles the solving side has exactly one move that pins a piece of the opponent: after the move a bishop, rook or queen of the solving side attacks an opponent piece that can't move away (or shouldn't), because the opponent's king or queen stands behind it on the same line.\n\nRules the puzzle must follow:\n- Exactly one legal move creates a new pin against the king or the queen. Pins that already exist before the move don't count, and no other move may create a pin as well.\n- After the move the pinning piece is safe: the opponent can't capture it, or only with a piece that is worth at least as much while the pinning piece is defended.\n- A pin against the queen only counts when the pinning piece is a bishop or rook.\n- There is no checkmate in 1 for the solving side.\n\nGuidelines:\n- Make the pin worth something: the pinned piece is undefended, attacked a second time, or the pin wins the piece behind it.\n- Keep positions compact and realistic, with 8 to 16 pieces in total, as if taken from a middlegame or endgame.\n- Use different pinning pieces (bishop, rook, queen) and both pins along files, ranks and diagonals.\n- Mix puzzles where white solves with puzzles where black solves.",
        "goal": "pin",
        "mate_in": null,
        "model": "claude-opus-5",
        "effort": "low",
        "is_active": true,
        "order": 4
    }
